🧠 First — the core problem CDN solves

Imagine your website's server is sitting in Mumbai 🇮🇳. Now imagine someone in New York 🇺🇸 tries to visit your site. Their request has to travel HALFWAY around the planet, get processed, and travel ALL THE WAY BACK. That's a LOT of physical distance = a LOT of delay (latency) ⏳

Speed of light is fast, but not infinite — data still takes real time to travel thousands of kilometers, bounce through routers, etc.

🌐 CDN (Content Delivery Network) — "Franchise stores instead of one HQ"

A CDN is basically a network of servers spread all across the globe that store COPIES of your website's static content (images, videos, CSS, JS files, etc).

Instead of EVERY user request traveling all the way to your main server in Mumbai, the CDN serves them from the closest possible location to them.

🎬 Real-life analogy: McDonald's vs a single restaurant 🍔

Imagine McDonald's had only ONE restaurant in the entire world, located in Mumbai. Everyone globally would have to fly to Mumbai just to get a burger 😩 — insane wait times.

Instead, McDonald's has franchises everywhere — Delhi, New York, London, Tokyo. You just walk to your NEAREST branch, and get the SAME burger, way faster. 🍔⚡

That's EXACTLY what a CDN does with your website's content — it opens up "franchise branches" (called edge servers / PoPs - Points of Presence) all over the world, each holding a cached copy of your content.